# goclaw host configuration - copy to .env and fill in.
# These map 1:1 to internal/config/config.go. All have defaults except the
# Telegram token, so the only thing you MUST set to take messages is that.
#
# .env is gitignored; .env.example is committed as the template.

# --- Channels ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather (https://t.me/BotFather → /newbot).
# Leave empty to run the host with no channels registered.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# --- First-run bootstrap ----------------------------------------------------

# Your Telegram numeric user id. Seeded at startup as the "owner" user so you
# get past the access gate without hand-editing the DB. Find it by messaging the
# bot and reading the `sender=` field in the host log.
GOCLAW_OWNER_TELEGRAM_ID=

# Set to 1 so that when the owner messages an as-yet-unwired chat, the host
# auto-wires that chat to the default agent group. Convenience for first-run;
# real deployments wire conversations explicitly. Default: off.
GOCLAW_AUTO_WIRE_OWNER=

# Name + folder of the default agent group seeded at startup (used by auto-wire).
# Defaults: default / default
GOCLAW_DEFAULT_AGENT_GROUP=
GOCLAW_DEFAULT_AGENT_GROUP_FOLDER=

# --- Storage ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Root directory for the central DB and per-session inbound/outbound DBs.
# Default: data
GOCLAW_DATA_DIR=data

# --- Security ---------------------------------------------------------------

# External mount allowlist (brief §6.3). Lives OUTSIDE the project root and is
# never itself mounted. If the file is absent the host fails closed (no extra
# mounts). Default: ~/.config/goclaw/mount-allowlist.json
GOCLAW_MOUNT_ALLOWLIST=

# --- Runtime ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Podman binary name or path. Default: podman
GOCLAW_PODMAN_BIN=podman

# Set to 1 so the host launches a Podman runner container per agent group on
# enqueue. Build an image first (echo stub OR the real Claude runner):
#   podman build -f container/runner.Containerfile -t goclaw-runner:latest .   # echo stub
#   podman build -f container/claude.Containerfile -t goclaw-claude:latest .   # real Claude
# When unset, start a runner out of band instead (go run ./cmd/stub-runner).
GOCLAW_LAUNCH_RUNNER=

# Runner image to launch. Default: goclaw-runner:latest (the echo stub).
# Use goclaw-claude:latest for the real Claude runner.
GOCLAW_RUNNER_IMAGE=

# --- Claude runner (only for goclaw-claude:latest) --------------------------
# Pick ONE. The API key is preferred (long-lived); if both are set, it wins.

# (a) RECOMMENDED - standard Anthropic API key (sk-ant-api…) from
# console.anthropic.com, passed in as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Long-lived: it does not
# expire, so the runner keeps working. Billed as API credits.
GOCLAW_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# (b) Claude Code subscription token, passed in as CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN.
# Bills against your Claude plan, BUT this token is SHORT-LIVED (~12h) and the
# container cannot refresh it - it WILL eventually fail with a 401, and you must
# re-extract it. On macOS the token lives in the Keychain (no file); extract with:
#   security find-generic-password -s "Claude Code-credentials" -w \
#     | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["claudeAiOauth"]["accessToken"])'
# (A Claude Code OAuth token, sk-ant-oat…, does NOT work in (a) - the API rejects it.)
GOCLAW_CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Knowledge vault (optional) ---------------------------------------------

# Host directory of the knowledge vault, mounted read-write at /vault in the
# agent-group container. The runner reads /vault/CLAUDE.md as its system prompt
# and can read/write notes (brief §11). Create one with: goclaw vault init
# Supports a leading ~ (e.g. ~/Vault). Empty disables the vault.
GOCLAW_VAULT_DIR=

# Git identity for the agent inside the container, so `git commit` works on the
# vault and on cloned repos. Defaults: "goclaw agent" / agent@goclaw.local
GOCLAW_GIT_USER_NAME=
GOCLAW_GIT_USER_EMAIL=
